BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gallery-route-one-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261010T121220Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Gallery Route One: Fellowship Program 2027-2028 (Point Re
 yes Station\, Bay Area artists)
DESCRIPTION:Gallery Route One\, a nonprofit artist-run gallery in Point Rey
 es Station\, offers a two-year fellowship for emerging artists from the gr
 eater Bay Area. WHAT YOU GET: USD 1\,500 toward the production and present
 ation of work\; a minimum of ten exhibition opportunities across the 24 mo
 nths\, including one solo show and one two-person exhibition\; mentoring f
 rom GRO artists\; publicity\, receptions\, artist talks\, radio appearance
 s and studio visits\; website and social media exposure\; professional dev
 elopment\; networking with artists\, donors and the community\; and eligib
 ility to teach in GRO's Artist in the Schools Program. ELIGIBILITY: artist
 s aged 21 to 40 who live or work in the greater Bay Area within commuting 
 distance of Point Reyes Station\, with a serious fine arts practice built 
 on training\, education or experience\, a portfolio of work from the last 
 five years\, and clear goals for artistic development and public exposure.
  Two fellows are selected each cycle. Applications are accepted 1 to 30 Se
 ptember 2026 via the form on the fellowship page. DEADLINE: 30 September 2
 026.\n\nOrganisation: Gallery Route One\nLocation: Gallery Route One\, 111
 01 Highway One\, Point Reyes Station\, California\, USA. Applicants must l
 ive or work in the greater Bay Area within commuting distance of Point Rey
 es Station.\nAward: Two fellowships. Each fellow receives USD 1\,500 to su
 pport the production and presentation of work\, a minimum of ten exhibitio
 n opportunities over two years including one solo show and one two-person 
 exhibition\, mentoring\, publicity and public engagement support\, and eli
 gibility to teach in the Artist in the Schools Program. No application fee
 .\nDuration: 24 months\, January 2027 to December 2028.\n\nMore: https://g
 alleryrouteone.org/programs/fellowship-program/fellowship-program-applicat
 ion/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://galleryrouteone.org/programs/fellowship-program/fellowship-prog
 ram-application/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Gallery Route One\, 11101 Highway One\, Point Reyes Station\, Cali
 fornia\, USA. Applicants must live or work in the greater Bay Area within 
 commuting distance of Point Reyes Station.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aapi-fund-creative-catalyst-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261010T121220Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261015
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:Deadline: AAPI Fund: Creative Catalyst Fellowship 2027 (theme Belon
 ging)
DESCRIPTION:The Creative Catalyst Fellowship from the AAPI Civic Engagement
  Fund supports individual artists whose work engages the public on critica
 l issues facing Asian Americans and Pacific Islanders and can serve as a c
 atalyst for civic participation. Up to eight artists each receive USD 30\,
 000 to produce work with the potential to break through to broader audienc
 es and spark conversation around the 2027 theme\, Belonging: a sense of co
 mmunity\, home\, agency and the essential right for AAPI communities to fl
 ourish\, as a counterweight to racism and violence. Fellows join a yearlon
 g cohort (January to December 2027) convened for peer sharing and learning
 . REQUIRED: a virtual orientation\, the two-day in-person Fellows retreat 
 on 16 to 18 February 2027 (location to be finalised\; the AAPI Fund covers
  travel and accommodation)\, virtual check-ins through the year\, and a na
 rrative report\; showcasing at AAPI Fund events is optional. OFFERINGS bey
 ond the grant: the annual retreat connecting fellows with community work a
 nd the wider social justice movement\, a communications consultant to plac
 e local and national news stories\, digital advertising promoting the fina
 l products\, introductions to AAPI organisations\, exhibition placement su
 pport (priority to venues experienced with social justice themes)\, and re
 plica development such as posters\, books\, calendars and stickers. ELIGIB
 ILITY: individual artists only (no organisations or collectives)\, 18 or o
 lder\, US residency required\; there is no citizenship requirement\, but s
 elected fellows provide a W-9 or W-8BEN. Work must connect with AAPI commu
 nities. Eligible forms include visual art (printmaking\, illustration\, ph
 otography\, modular installations)\, digital media\, written forms and hyb
 rid work\; feature-length film\, fully staged theater\, dance works and la
 rge-scale fixed installations are ineligible\, though such practices can b
 e adapted into more concise formats. Previous fellows may reapply three ye
 ars after completing their fellowship. APPLICATION: via the online form wi
 th up to five examples of work relating to the proposed project as a singl
 e PDF (portfolio or website links allowed). Deadline 15 October 2026 at 11
 :59pm PT\; fellowship announcement 14 January 2027. Artwork must be comple
 ted by 15 August 2027. Contact: creative@aapifund.org with subject CC inqu
 iry.\n\nOrganisation: AAPI Civic Engagement Fund\nLocation: United States\
 ; US residency required\, no citizenship requirement (W-9 or W-8BEN needed
 ). In-person retreat 16 to 18 February 2027\, travel and accommodation cov
 ered.\nAward: Up to eight artists each receive USD 30\,000 to produce new 
 work. Also covered: the two-day Fellows retreat (travel and accommodation 
 paid by the AAPI Fund)\, an accomplished communications consultant\, digit
 al advertising\, community introductions\, exhibition placement support an
 d replica development (posters\, books\, calendars) promoting the finished
  work. No application fee.\nDuration: Yearlong fellowship\, January to Dec
 ember 2027\; artwork completed by 15 August 2027\, promotion of completed 
 work August to December 2027.\n\nMore: https://aapifund.org/2027-creative-
 catalyst-fellowship-open-call/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://aapifund.org/2027-creative-catalyst-fellowship-open-call/?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States\; US residency required\, no citizenship requirement
  (W-9 or W-8BEN needed). In-person retreat 16 to 18 February 2027\, travel
  and accommodation covered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:princeton-arts-fellowship-2027-2029@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261010T121220Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: Princeton Arts Fellowship 2027-2029 (Lewis Center for the
  Arts)
DESCRIPTION:The Princeton Arts Fellowship places early-career artists at th
 e Lewis Center for the Arts for two academic years on a full-time salaried
  appointment\, on the conviction that exposure to the arts helps us make s
 ense of our lives and the lives of our neighbours. ELIGIBLE APPLICANTS are
  early-career composers\, conductors\, musicians\, choreographers\, playwr
 ights\, designers\, directors and performance artists. IMPORTANT FOR THIS 
 CYCLE: applications are accepted only for the Program in Dance\, the Progr
 am in Theater and Music Theater\, and the Department of Music. Fellows typ
 ically teach one undergraduate course per semester (subject to approval by
  the Office of the Dean of the Faculty)\, or in some cases take on an arti
 stic assignment with students in lieu of a class\; prior teaching experien
 ce is NOT required. Beyond that\, fellows are expected to engage with the 
 Princeton community through advising students\, talks\, workshops\, exhibi
 tions or other events\, and have time to pursue their own artistic practic
 e and scholarly research. NOT ELIGIBLE: the appointment cannot be used to 
 fund work leading to a PhD or any advanced degree\; holders of Princeton P
 hDs cannot apply\; past recipients of the Hodder Fellowship cannot apply\;
  and anyone with a sustained and continuous relationship with Princeton is
  excluded (an occasional and sporadic relationship is fine). Fellows may n
 ot hold concurrent teaching positions at other institutions during the app
 ointment. You may apply once per application cycle. APPLICATION via Interf
 olio: a CV\; a 750-word proposal broken into how you would use the fellows
 hip to develop your work (250 words)\, what you might teach or pursue with
  undergraduates (250 words)\, and your approach to building community with
  people of different backgrounds\, experiences\, perspectives and artistic
  practices through your practice\, teaching and/or research (250 words)\; 
 contact information for one reference\; and work samples with a 150-word s
 tatement on how they relate to your proposal\, submitted as links in a PDF
 . WORK SAMPLE SPECS: Dance (choreography)\, one to three links to performa
 nces\, rehearsals or research\, continuous footage only\, no trailers\, ph
 otographs or promotional material\, with cue points of no more than 3 to 4
  minutes per link and no more than 10 minutes total. Music\, one to two sc
 ores and/or links to performance examples totalling 10 minutes\, video opt
 ional. Theater\, up to 15 pages of writing sample and/or up to 10 design i
 mages (photos or renderings) and/or a video or audio reel with cue points\
 , no more than 10 minutes to watch. The deadline is 8 September 2026 at 11
 :59 pm EDT\; receipt of applications cannot be confirmed and late applicat
 ions are not accepted. FAQ and examples of building community in artistic 
 practice\, teaching and research: arts.princeton.edu/fellowships/faq.\n\nO
 rganisation: Princeton University\, Lewis Center for the Arts\nLocation: I
 n person on campus at Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA. Appointees are expecte
 d to be full and active members of the community with frequent\, engaged i
 nteraction with students. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship
  of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing.\nAward: USD 93\,000 salary
  per year for each of two academic years\, as a full-time 24-month appoint
 ment at the rank of Associate Professional Specialist. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Two academic years\, 2027-2028 and 2028-2029. Applications ope
 n 1 July 2026 and close 8 September 2026\; finalists attend an on-campus i
 nterview during the week of 7 December 2026.\n\nMore: https://apply.interf
 olio.com/187043?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://apply.interfolio.com/187043?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person on campus at Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA. Appointees ar
 e expected to be full and active members of the community with frequent\, 
 engaged interaction with students. These positions are not eligible for sp
 onsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:beinecke-short-term-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261010T121220Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Beinecke Library Short-Term Research Fellowships 2027-202
 8 (Yale)
DESCRIPTION:The Beinecke Library Fellowship Program offers competitive shor
 t-term fellowships to support research projects that substantively engage 
 with Yale Library Special Collections materials. WHO CAN APPLY: academic a
 nd independent scholars\, locally and globally\, with or without an instit
 utional affiliation\; the library explicitly welcomes applicants regardles
 s of institutional association\, race\, cultural background\, ability\, se
 xual orientation\, gender or socioeconomic status. Applications using trad
 itional archival and bibliographic research methods are encouraged\, and s
 o are creative\, interdisciplinary and non-traditional approaches to worki
 ng with the collections\, which makes this a live option for artists and w
 riters whose practice is research-driven. WHAT YOU GET: USD 5\,000 per mon
 th for an onsite research visit of between two weeks and two months\, paid
  at the start of the fellowship whenever possible (international fellows s
 hould expect payment to take several weeks)\; fellows pay their own taxes 
 on the award. TIMELINE: applications for the 2027-2028 awards opened 1 Jun
 e 2026 and close 15 August 2026\, via Interfolio\; all decisions are notif
 ied by 31 January 2027\; research must be conducted between 1 July 2027 an
 d 30 June 2028\, and awards cannot be deferred.\n\nOrganisation: Beinecke 
 Rare Book & Manuscript Library\, Yale University\nLocation: Onsite at Yale
  Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connecticut\, USA. Open to acad
 emic and independent scholars locally and globally\, regardless of institu
 tional association.\nAward: USD 5\,000 per month for research visits betwe
 en two weeks and two months. Funding is paid at the beginning of the fello
 wship where possible (payment to international fellows often takes several
  weeks)\; fellows are responsible for taxes on the award. Free to apply.\n
 Duration: Two weeks to two months of onsite research\, conducted between 1
  July 2027 and 30 June 2028\; awards cannot be deferred\n\nMore: https://b
 einecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/short-term?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://beinecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/shor
 t-term?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Onsite at Yale Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connectic
 ut\, USA. Open to academic and independent scholars locally and globally\,
  regardless of institutional association.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nrdc-climate-storytelling-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261010T121220Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261204
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261205
SUMMARY:Deadline: NRDC Climate Storytelling Fellowship 2027 (via The Black 
 List)
DESCRIPTION:Annual screenwriting fellowship from the Natural Resources Defe
 nse Council (NRDC) administered through The Black List\, awarding $20\,000
  each to three writers to revise a feature screenplay or pilot that engage
 s with climate change in a compelling way. Submission and selection happen
  on The Black List platform\; applicants should review program eligibility
  and any associated hosting/submission fees on blcklst.com before applying
 . Strong fit for narrative writers using fiction to dramatise climate\, ec
 ology\, energy\, or environmental-justice themes (rather than documentary)
 . 2027 CYCLE: submissions are open until 4 December 2026 and are capped at
  a maximum of 500\, so early entry is advised. All accepted submissions re
 ceive a free script evaluation and one free month of script hosting on the
  platform. Fellows also receive consultation with NRDC's Rewrite the Futur
 e team\, which works to transform the onscreen portrayal of climate change
  beyond dystopia and apocalypse toward solutions\, plus six months of ment
 orship from an established screenwriter\; past mentors include Lucia Aniel
 lo\, Dustin Lance Black\, Roxane Gay\, Nick Kroll and Daniel Scheinert.\n\
 nOrganisation: Natural Resources Defense Council (NRDC) in partnership wit
 h The Black List\nLocation: United States (verify international eligibilit
 y on Black List program page)\nAward: $20\,000 to each of three writers ($
 60\,000 total) to support revision of a feature screenplay or pilot.\nDura
 tion: Per project (revision phase of feature screenplay or pilot)\n\nMore:
  https://blcklst.com/programs/2027-nrdc-climate-storytelling-fellowship?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://blcklst.com/programs/2027-nrdc-climate-storytelling-fellowship?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States (verify international eligibility on Black List prog
 ram page)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:film-independent-screenwriting-lab-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261010T121220Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Film Independent: Screenwriting Lab 2027
DESCRIPTION:Competitive screenwriting lab for emerging feature screenwriter
 s. Selected fellows are eligible for the Climate Entertainment Development
  Grant ($25K\, climate-focused fiction features) and the same bundled $10K
  fellowship pool available across Artist Development programs (Cayton-Gold
 rich\, MPAC Hollywood Bureau\, Sony Music Vision). International fellows a
 re also eligible for the Dolby Institute Fellowship ($50K post-production 
 grant utilising Dolby Vision and Atmos). Lab alums become eligible to appl
 y to the Amplifier Fellowship for Black filmmakers ($30K unrestricted plus
  year-long support\; six fellows annually). Applications open 29 June 2026
 \; non-member deadline 31 August 2026\; Film Independent member extension 
 to 14 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Film Independent\nLocation: Los Ang
 eles\, US\nAward: Lab fellowship + access to bundled $10K fellowships (Cay
 ton-Goldrich\, MPAC\, Sony Music Vision) and Climate Entertainment Develop
 ment Grant ($25K for climate-focused fiction features)\nDuration: Lab coho
 rt\n\nMore: https://www.filmindependent.org/programs/applications/?utm_sou
 rce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.filmindependent.org/programs/applications/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Los Angeles\, US
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:macdowell-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261010T121220Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: MacDowell Fellowship
DESCRIPTION:Residency for artists across seven disciplines (architecture\, 
 film/video\, interdisciplinary\, literature\, music composition\, theatre\
 , visual arts). Sole selection criterion is artistic excellence. Applicati
 ons open 15 August 2026. February deadline of the following year covers th
 e Fall/Winter cycle.\n\nOrganisation: MacDowell\nLocation: Peterborough\, 
 New Hampshire\, USA\nAward: No residency fee\; need-based stipends and tra
 vel reimbursement available\; ~300 fellowships/year\nDuration: Up to 8 wee
 ks (Spring/Summer 2027 cycle: 1 March to 31 August 2027)\n\nMore: https://
 www.macdowell.org/apply/apply-for-fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.macdowell.org/apply/apply-for-fellowship?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Peterborough\, New Hampshire\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:princeton-hodder-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261010T121220Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Hodder Fellowship 2027-2028 (Princeton University)
DESCRIPTION:The Hodder Fellowship is given to artists and writers of except
 ional promise to pursue independent projects at Princeton University durin
 g the academic year. Potential Hodder Fellows are composers\, choreographe
 rs\, performance artists\, visual artists\, writers\, translators\, or oth
 er kinds of artists or humanists who have 'much more than ordinary intelle
 ctual and literary gifts'\; they are selected more 'for promise than for p
 erformance.' Given the strength of the applicant pool\, most successful Fe
 llows have published a first book or have similar achievements in their ow
 n fields\; the Hodder is designed to provide Fellows with the 'studious le
 isure' to undertake significant new work. Fellows spend an academic year a
 t Princeton with no formal teaching\, supported by a USD 93\,000 stipend f
 or the 10-month Visiting Fellow appointment. IMPORTANT: for this cycle\, a
 pplications are accepted only for the Program in Dance\, the Program in Th
 eater and Music Theater\, the Program in Visual Arts\, and the Department 
 of Music. Fellowships are not intended to fund work leading to an advanced
  degree\; one need not be a U.S. citizen to apply\; previous recipients of
  the Princeton Arts Fellowship are not eligible. To apply\, submit a resum
 e\, a 500-word project proposal\, and samples of your work (writing sample
 \, images\, video links to performances\, etc.). Applicants may apply for 
 the Hodder Fellowship only twice in a lifetime. Applications are submitted
  via Interfolio. The application cycle closes 8 September 2026 at 11:59 p.
 m. EDT.\n\nOrganisation: Princeton University\, Lewis Center for the Arts\
 nLocation: Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA\; academic-year appointment as a V
 isiting Fellow (no U.S. citizenship required)\nAward: USD 93\,000 stipend 
 for the 10-month appointment as a Visiting Fellow. No formal teaching is i
 nvolved. No application fee.\nDuration: One academic year (10-month appoin
 tment)\, 2027-2028\n\nMore: https://arts.princeton.edu/fellowships/hodder-
 fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arts.princeton.edu/fellowships/hodder-fellowship/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA\; academic-year appointment as a Visi
 ting Fellow (no U.S. citizenship required)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bavc-mediamaker-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261010T121220Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: BAVC MediaMaker Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The BAVC MediaMaker Fellowship is a ten-month intensive for doc
 umentary filmmakers using bold cinematic language and innovative impact st
 rategies to take on critical issues of the moment. Each fellow receives US
 D 10\,000 in unrestricted funding paid out across the programme\, plus tra
 vel per diems\, and joins a cohort programme of workshops\, feedback sessi
 ons\, mentorship and industry access rather than a one-off cash award. ELI
 GIBILITY: directors of a first or second feature-length documentary\, base
 d primarily in the United States. The project must be non-commercial and f
 eature-length (shorts\, series and podcasts are excluded)\, primarily docu
 mentary or non-fiction including hybrid forms\, and demonstrate potential 
 for social impact. Only one applicant per filmmaking team may apply\, and 
 full-time students are not eligible. PROJECT STAGE: eligible films must ha
 ve at least begun production and may have started post-production\, but sh
 ould not reach a fine cut before the programme year ends\, since the point
  is to workshop the film in progress. APPLICATION: the first step is an In
 tent to Apply\, submitted through the Airtable form linked from the progra
 mme page\, due 30 August 2026. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: BAVC Media (Bay Ar
 ea Video Coalition)\nLocation: United States\; applicants must primarily r
 eside in the U.S. and not expect to live abroad for a major part of the ye
 ar. Mostly remote\, with two in-person convenings in San Francisco (travel
  covered).\nAward: USD 10\,000 distributed to each Fellow over the course 
 of the fellowship\, plus travel per diems. Non-cash support includes fligh
 ts and accommodation for two San Francisco convenings\, festival and confe
 rence access with travel covered\, professional mentorship and strategy se
 ssions\, five hours of one-to-one mentorship through MediaMaker Connect\, 
 and ongoing workshops. Bay Area fellows also get access to BAVC's co-worki
 ng space and facilities. No application fee.\nDuration: Ten-month fellowsh
 ip\n\nMore: https://bavc.org/programs/mediamaker/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://bavc.org/programs/mediamaker/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States\; applicants must primarily reside in the U.S. and n
 ot expect to live abroad for a major part of the year. Mostly remote\, wit
 h two in-person convenings in San Francisco (travel covered).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:laselle-travel-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261010T121220Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Travel Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Dorothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation established this fell
 owship in recognition of the impact travel can have on an artist's career.
  Two awards of USD 5\,000 go to artists working and residing in Texas\, en
 abling each recipient to complete a research trip that furthers their arti
 stic practice. The fellowship echoes its namesake's own history: Dorothy A
 ntoinette 'Toni' LaSelle\, a pioneer of Modernism in Texas\, travelled to 
 Europe in 1927-28 after completing her MA at the University of Chicago\, e
 xploring the art of England\, Italy and France on a trip made possible by 
 a study fund awarded for work in the First Student Art Exhibition at the R
 enaissance Society in 1926. JUROR: this year's guest juror is Lana Meador\
 , Associate Curator of Modern and Contemporary Art at the San Antonio Muse
 um of Art\, who joined SAMA's curatorial department in 2015 and has organi
 sed more than a dozen exhibitions there\, including co-curating Texas Wome
 n: A New History of Abstract Art (2020) and the ongoing Gateway series of 
 commissioned site-specific murals. She has juried artist awards for the Ci
 ty of San Antonio's Department of Arts and Culture\, the International Col
 or Awards\, Texas Sculpture Group and the Utah Artist Fellowship. ELIGIBIL
 ITY: must currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year
 \; must be a practising professional with at least three solo or group exh
 ibitions in the past three years (BFA and juried exhibitions count)\; and 
 must currently hold a degree in the arts. APPLICATION MATERIALS: image thu
 mbnails of a maximum of four works with title\, date\, medium and dimensio
 ns (JPEG or JPG only\, at least 1\,200 pixels on the longest side and unde
 r 5MB each)\; an artist CV\; a reference contact list with two names\, con
 tact details and relationship to the artist\; a project proposal of 2\,000
  characters or less covering why the fellowship matters to your practice\,
  your goals for the travel\, and when the proposed travel would take place
 \; and a copy of a valid Texas driver's licence or other proof of one year
  of Texas residency. All information and communications are in English\, w
 ith translation assistance available on request. Applications opened 3 Aug
 ust 2026\; awardees are notified 2 November 2026. Questions: info@lasellef
 oundation.org. Deadline 31 August 2026 at 11:59pm CDT.\n\nOrganisation: Do
 rothy Antoinette LaSelle Foundation\nLocation: Texas\, USA. Applicants mus
 t currently reside in Texas and have lived there for the past year. The fu
 nded research trip itself may be anywhere.\nAward: Two awards of USD 5\,00
 0 each\, distributed in the fall of 2026\, to fund a research trip that fu
 rthers the artist's practice. No application fee.\nDuration: Travel must b
 e completed between 1 December 2026 and 31 December 2027\n\nMore: https://
 www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.lasellefoundation.org/travel-fellowship?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Texas\, USA. Applicants must currently reside in Texas and have li
 ved there for the past year. The funded research trip itself may be anywhe
 re.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asf-fellowships-americans-nordic-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261010T121220Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: American-Scandinavian Foundation: Fellowships for America
 ns in the Nordic Countries 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:The American-Scandinavian Foundation's award program for study 
 and research abroad is its longest-standing commitment to educational exch
 ange: over the past 100 years\, more than 4\,000 fellowships and grants ha
 ve gone to Americans working in the Nordic region and Scandinavians in the
  US engaged in study\, research\, or creative arts projects. Fellowships f
 or Americans in the Nordic Countries support projects in Denmark\, the Far
 oe Islands\, Greenland\, Iceland\, Finland\, Norway\, Sweden\, and Sapmi. 
 AMOUNTS: long-term fellowships of up to USD 23\,000 for 4 to 12 months\, a
 nd short-term grants of up to USD 5\,000 for 1 to 3 months\; awards are gi
 ven in all fields\, with graduate students (preferably dissertation-relate
 d)\, academic professionals\, and creative artists eligible. In 2026\, 31 
 American Fellows were granted over USD 277\,390 in total. REPORTING: fello
 ws abroad more than six months submit an interim report mid-project\, and 
 all fellows submit a final narrative report within 60 days of the fellowsh
 ip period's end. DEADLINE: 1 November 2026 for the 2027-28 cycle. See ASF'
 s Program Guidelines for full requirements and apply via the ASF applicati
 on portal.\n\nOrganisation: American-Scandinavian Foundation\nLocation: No
 rdic region (Denmark\, Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Iceland\, Finland\, Nor
 way\, Sweden\, and Sapmi). For American graduate students\, academic profe
